{
  "sn53.1-12:0.1": "Linked Discourses 53.1–12 ",
  "sn53.1-12:0.2": "1. Abbreviated Texts on the Ganges ",
  "sn53.1-12:0.3": "Absorptions, Etc. ",
  "sn53.1-12:1.1": "At Sāvatthī. ",
  "sn53.1-12:1.2": "",
  "sn53.1-12:1.3": "“Mendicants, there are these four absorptions. ",
  "sn53.1-12:1.4": "What four? ",
  "sn53.1-12:1.5": "It’s when a mendicant, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. ",
  "sn53.1-12:1.6": "As the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, they enter and remain in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. ",
  "sn53.1-12:1.7": "And with the fading away of rapture, they enter and remain in the third absorption, where they meditate with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss.’ ",
  "sn53.1-12:1.8": "With the giving up of pleasure and pain and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, they enter and remain in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness. ",
  "sn53.1-12:1.9": "These are the four absorptions. ",
  "sn53.1-12:2.1": "The Ganges River slants, slopes, and inclines to the east. ",
  "sn53.1-12:2.2":